## 4. (c) against the engine's own raw replicate-level test in the published reports
Where (d) estimated the effect, its report holds the raw test at the replicate level (raw_detected,
p_raw; the same data without the correction). (c) runs that test; with an exact permutation they must agree.
  exact permutation or t interval: 2091 agree, 0 differ; Monte Carlo permutation (N7): 251, the same detection at alpha on 248 (their p differ by the Monte Carlo error: other draws)
  (N6a's random metric is not compared: it draws a new number at every call, so its values depend on
  the calls made before the effect, and (d) made GATE 0's, 4's and 5's first)

## 5. Where (b) and (d) disagree on null data (decided after the results)
A false 'effect' of (b) only is one the gates removed; of (d) only, one they made. For the latter, the
report says whether the engine's raw replicate-level test (the uncorrected data) detected it, and how much
of the raw difference the correction kept (retained > 1: the correction enlarged it).
